//
// Expected: c1 at one session and at two.
//
// A derivation is refused only when no execution produces it, not when the
// one route the closure happened to record is incoherent.
//
// The attacker swaps Bob's KEM encapsulation key for its own, which gives
// up the shared secret but also fails the tag Alice checks after she has
// already sent the message. Alice halts there, so she never reaches her
// own leak and her ephemeral is withheld. Bob checks nothing, reaches
// phase 1 and gives up bx, and DH_KEX(gbx, ae) is the same term as
// DH_KEX(gae, bx), so the Diffie-Hellman leg is still open.
//
// The closure keeps one derivation per value and dedupes by equivalence,
// so the route it records for that leg reads ae, the ephemeral the halted
// run never discloses. Refusing on that route alone loses the attack even
// though bx rebuilds the same value. `combination_coheres` therefore
// restricts knowledge to what the replayed execution reaches and asks
// whether the derivation still goes through there.
//
// Guarding gbx is what makes bx the only way through: with it open the
// attacker replaces both keys and needs no leak at all.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows public c0
]

principal Bob[
	knows private bx, dkb
	gbx = PUBKEY(bx)
	ekb = PUBKEY(dkb)
	tag = HASH(ekb)
]

Bob -> Alice: [gbx], ekb, [tag]

principal Alice[
	generates ae, r, m, n
	gae = PUBKEY(ae)
	s, ct = KEM_ENCAP(ekb, r)
	k = HASH(DH_KEX(gbx, ae), s)
	e = AEAD_ENC(k, n, m, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: gae, ct, n, e

principal Alice[
	_ = ASSERT(HASH(ekb), tag)?
]

phase[1]

principal Alice[leaks ae]
principal Bob[leaks bx]

queries[
	confidentiality? m
]
